Z||000008||Oreif||09-01-2001||10:46 AM||||There are 2 ways to strengthen the chassis. 1. is the "X" brace on the bottom. Problem is if you need to drop the tank or change any of the front to back lines (brakes, coolant, etc) it becomes a pain.<P>2. is how Bob Steiger at Twin Lakes does it.<BR>He drives stock square steel beams thru the frame rails and welds them to the front and rear shock/strut towers. Coyote has a chassis with the top cut off. He was going to make a speedster/convertable but his GT project has higher priority. Anyhow, The frame is already modified. You can jump on the center of the car and there is no flex.<BR>(He may be willing to sell the rolling chassis if someone else wants to make a speedster)<P>Number 2 is the best for the fact nothing gets blocked. Not sure what the strength differences are.||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfixit58||09-01-2001||11:01 AM||||Oreif,<P>The only problem I see with only using the rocker panel supports is there is little to no torsional support. It may work well for support in the up and down direction but if you shove the nose into a corner hard, the body may twist. It may no twist enough to do any permanent damage but will screw with the handling. Mind you, that sine I've never ridden in the type you've discribed this is just half theoretical. I have ridden in Cozmo's targa and it felt firm with very little twist or flex. I think it was because of the "X" bracing.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||3
Z||000010||Oreif||09-01-2001||11:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfixit58:<BR><B>Oreif,<P>The only problem I see with only using the rocker panel supports is there is little to no torsional support. It may work well for support in the up and down direction but if you shove the nose into a corner hard, the body may twist. It may no twist enough to do any permanent damage but will screw with the handling. Mind you, that sine I've never ridden in the type you've discribed this is just half theoretical. I have ridden in Cozmo's targa and it felt firm with very little twist or flex. I think it was because of the "X" bracing.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I'm not sure of the twist type of flex in a corner, But Bob Steiger has a convertable 87 GT with a 3800SC that he's been driving for a about 5 years and never had a problem. (that I know of) Tying the front and rear sub-frames to each other does use the lateral supports of the cars original space frame. That is basically what both styles do. The frame rail goes all the way to the middle of each sub-frame and the "X" ties the front and rear frames where they join at the chassis. At Fierorama next week I'll look at Bob's car and see if there are any stress cracks and ask him how strong it is. Maybe we could get someone who's familiar with mechanical stress to look at both designs and see which would be better or at least verify both are good. I'd hate to see someone use either method and twist something so bad it wrecks the car.<BR>Does anyone know how GM did it on the convertable prototypes? How about how they do the F-bodies? They would have simular flex. I thought a few members had contacts at GM and could possibly snoop around for some info.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||09-01-2001||05:30 PM||||On F-bodies and LeBarons, they double up the floor pan and the quarter panels inside are different on convertible and coupe. Mercedes SL uses the same exact unibody as the sedan, they just shorten it and weld 3 floorpans together onto each other. there so rigid that you can jack up the left front and right rear and doors still close with fingertips. Thats the pre 90 models, I couldnt say about the later ones. o||||reg||1
Z||000012||BlackDragon||09-01-2001||06:10 PM||||what about putting a bolt-on carbon fiber undercarraige that ran from bumper to bumper and side to side for support?  that way you'd be able to take it off if you need to work on the underneath of the car.<P>------------------<BR>im looking for a inexpensive black standard fastback. does not have to run. does not have to look good. only the mid section needs to be dent free. the fenders,bumpers, dont have to be in good cond. faded paint is fine. anyone who has info, email me. grey interior would be nice but beggars cant be choosers||||reg||1
Z||000013||artherd||09-01-2001||07:43 PM||||Aus, have I told you lately that I love you?!? (and that car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AusFiero:<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rebody005.jpg">  <P><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000014||Cozmo||09-01-2001||08:49 PM||||Aus, that looks sweet!  I'll get some pics of the reinforcement as soon as my &*%$#$ friend returns my camera <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I'll try and describe what's there...<BR>There is a box made out of 1" square steel tube that is welded to the spaceframe just inside the cooling tubes from the rear wheelwell to the front.  The X-frame bolts to the box at the corners.  This lets you get at the tank etc.<BR>The car is a little creaky around the top when it's installed (crappy gaskets) but it handles pretty normally.  I've jacked it up numerous ways and have seen no door gap flexing.<BR>I have heard of the technique Orief mentioned, it seems like a pretty slick way to reinforce the car.  Maybe do that AND bolt in an X-frame for added torsional strength...<P>Roy, I've decided to go ahead and put the white Coupe body on the Targa (Thanks for the suggestion... I owe ya a beer... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>BobC||||reg||1
Z||000015||AusFiero||09-02-2001||03:55 AM||||Yeah Oriefs way was similar to what I was thinking. Over hee they tend to weld 1/8" plat to the exisiting floorpan and sills when making convertibles from cars that werent originally. And still add an X member.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000016||fierobaby||09-02-2001||09:47 AM||||I would love to do a targa top on my Fiero.. to much money though. but they look sweet I really like that rebody aus! its GREAT! I can see if my friend has any pics of the underside of his old Fiero.. I just found out he had a fiero with a targa... but sold it... It was a black one with red interior.. looked great from the pics I have seen. :-)||||reg||1
Z||000017||Songman||09-02-2001||10:53 AM||||This is a little off-topic really, but with imagination can be related...<P>I always read about the x-braces on old cars like my 69 Firebird convertible and how usually when you find a car the x-brace is missing. I saw pictures of the aftermarket x-braces in OPG and Year One and others but just assumed they were big.<P>Luckily my car had it's x-brace and I was surprised at how small it was! Of course, unibody is a little different than our space frame setup, but how big would a brace actually have to be? And why couldn't it be a bolt on application as mentioned above? It could actually turn into something pretty cool. Anyone ever seen the bottom of an F355? Totally smooth and has a wind tunnel down the middle of it....<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
